WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.248
Picture a busy Friday night in a restaurant.

2
00:00:05.248 --> 00:00:08.665
The manager's clipboard says four waiters are on the floor.

3
00:00:08.867 --> 00:00:09.897
And that's true.

4
00:00:09.897 --> 00:00:10.928
But look closer.

5
00:00:10.928 --> 00:00:15.244
One of them has stood frozen at the coffee machine for ten minutes.

6
00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:19.271
Another can't take orders, because their card reader is broken.

7
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.401
The count is right, and guests are still waiting.

8
00:00:22.401 --> 00:00:25.124
Being on the floor is not the same as working.

9
00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:27.604
Now the same scene in Kubernetes.

10
00:00:27.604 --> 00:00:29.875
It tells you your Pod is Running.

11
00:00:29.875 --> 00:00:31.114
So your app works.

12
00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:31.527
Right?

13
00:00:31.527 --> 00:00:33.729
Let's see if that's really true.

14
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:36.948
Kubernetes already handles one kind of trouble.

15
00:00:36.948 --> 00:00:40.347
When a container's process exits, it starts it again.

16
00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:42.525
But our two waiters didn't crash.

17
00:00:42.525 --> 00:00:45.551
From the outside, they look just like healthy ones.

18
00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:50.695
Our lab app, hello-devops, keeps its visit count in Redis, a small database.

19
00:00:50.695 --> 00:00:52.835
A stuck app is the frozen waiter.

20
00:00:52.835 --> 00:00:57.051
An app that's up but can't reach Redis is the broken card reader.

21
00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:00.281
Honestly, Running should have been called: has a pulse.

22
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.771
It only tells you the process exists.

23
00:01:02.771 --> 00:01:04.673
That's the clipboard's mistake.

24
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:11.584
Wouldn't it be nice if the manager walked the floor, and actually asked each waiter a question, every so often?

25
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.199
Walk over, ask, listen.

26
00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:18.264
Let's map that.

27
00:01:18.264 --> 00:01:22.386
The manager is the kubelet, the helper program on each node.

28
00:01:22.386 --> 00:01:24.379
The waiter is your container.

29
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.780
The question is a probe: a small check on a timer.

30
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.613
In the lab, the kubelet asks the container for a web address, like /healthz.

31
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:38.645
The answer is a status code, the number a web server sends back.

32
00:01:38.645 --> 00:01:40.551
200 to 399 is a pass.

33
00:01:40.551 --> 00:01:42.731
503 means not available.

34
00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:45.252
One question isn't enough.

35
00:01:45.252 --> 00:01:49.349
A good manager asks three, and each answer has its own action.

36
00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:53.925
First, to a new waiter still tying an apron: have you finished starting?

37
00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:59.977
Until it's yes, the other questions wait, so a slow start isn't mistaken for a stuck one.

38
00:01:59.977 --> 00:02:02.275
If yes never comes, it's restarted.

39
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.899
Second, to everyone, every so often: are you stuck?

40
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:10.864
After enough no's in a row, the waiter goes home and a fresh one comes in.

41
00:02:10.864 --> 00:02:11.959
That's a restart.

42
00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:14.648
Third: can you take tables right now?

43
00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:18.090
While it's no, no new tables go to that waiter.

44
00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.531
But nobody goes home.

45
00:02:19.733 --> 00:02:24.369
Now the names: the startup probe, the liveness probe, and the readiness probe.

46
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.769
Three questions, three actions: wait, restart, or stop sending visitors.

47
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.505
Our app has two addresses for this.

48
00:02:32.505 --> 00:02:35.725
/healthz answers while the process can serve.

49
00:02:35.725 --> 00:02:39.876
/readyz also checks Redis, and returns 503 when it's down.

50
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.064
How fast does each one act?

51
00:02:43.064 --> 00:02:46.853
Multiply how often it checks by how many misses it allows.

52
00:02:46.853 --> 00:02:53.843
The lab's startup probe checks every two seconds, and allows thirty failures: up to sixty seconds to start.

53
00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:54.735
Your turn.

54
00:02:54.735 --> 00:02:59.550
Liveness checks every ten seconds, and acts after three misses in a row.

55
00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:02.559
How long does a stuck container keep its job?

56
00:03:02.559 --> 00:03:03.629
Pause and guess.

57
00:03:05.833 --> 00:03:07.284
About thirty seconds.

58
00:03:07.284 --> 00:03:09.772
Miss, miss, miss, and it's replaced.

59
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.916
Readiness checks every five seconds, and acts after two misses.

60
00:03:13.916 --> 00:03:17.459
So a Pod stops getting visitors within about ten seconds.

61
00:03:18.267 --> 00:03:19.587
Now a tempting idea.

62
00:03:19.587 --> 00:03:22.559
If checking is good, more checking is better.

63
00:03:22.559 --> 00:03:25.135
So let's make /healthz check Redis too.

64
00:03:25.367 --> 00:03:27.933
Liveness now asks about Redis as well.

65
00:03:27.933 --> 00:03:29.689
Sounds responsible, right?

66
00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.808
Picture Redis going down, for just one minute.

67
00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:36.999
Every hello-devops Pod fails liveness at about the same time.

68
00:03:36.999 --> 00:03:39.404
And the kubelet restarts all of them.

69
00:03:39.633 --> 00:03:41.459
But restarts can't fix Redis.

70
00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:44.984
And while they happen, no Pod can serve anything at all.

71
00:03:44.984 --> 00:03:47.376
A small outage just became a full one.

72
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:54.245
It's the manager who hears the card network is down for the whole street, and sends every waiter home.

73
00:03:54.245 --> 00:03:54.702
Well...

74
00:03:54.702 --> 00:03:57.177
nobody's card reader is a problem now.

75
00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.770
So the point is: a restart only fixes trouble inside the container.

76
00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:07.248
The docs warn this can lead to cascading failures, one failure setting off the next.

77
00:04:07.967 --> 00:04:10.621
Liveness asks about the container alone.

78
00:04:10.621 --> 00:04:12.545
Leave the database out of it.

79
00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:18.235
Checks on other services belong in readiness, because failing it only pauses visitors.

80
00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:20.689
Let's try it the way the lab does.

81
00:04:20.689 --> 00:04:21.997
Scale Redis to zero.

82
00:04:21.997 --> 00:04:26.508
Pause and guess: what happens to READY, and what happens to RESTARTS?

83
00:04:28.733 --> 00:04:30.598
READY drops to zero of one.

84
00:04:30.598 --> 00:04:32.393
STATUS still says Running.

85
00:04:32.393 --> 00:04:34.257
And RESTARTS stays at zero.

86
00:04:34.467 --> 00:04:36.558
That quiet gap is the whole point.

87
00:04:36.558 --> 00:04:38.404
The waiters stay on the floor.

88
00:04:38.404 --> 00:04:40.311
They just don't get new tables.

89
00:04:40.533 --> 00:04:44.487
The failed check marks the Pod not ready, and the Service skips it.

90
00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:49.338
Scale Redis back to one, and the Pods become ready again, on their own.

91
00:04:49.338 --> 00:04:50.644
Nobody touched them.

92
00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:52.531
One honest limit.

93
00:04:52.531 --> 00:04:59.039
If every Pod fails readiness at once, the Service has no ready Pods left, and users get errors.

94
00:04:59.267 --> 00:05:04.927
So the lab's readiness probe isn't too strict: a two second timeout, and two failures.

95
00:05:05.733 --> 00:05:10.156
The moral of the story: being present is not the same as working.

96
00:05:10.156 --> 00:05:11.108
Startup waits.

97
00:05:11.108 --> 00:05:12.741
Liveness sends you home.

98
00:05:12.741 --> 00:05:14.510
Readiness steps you aside.

99
00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:17.436
And liveness never asks about the database.

100
00:05:18.267 --> 00:05:20.271
One quick check before you go.

101
00:05:20.271 --> 00:05:26.083
An app needs ninety seconds to start, and liveness kills it at thirty, again and again.

102
00:05:26.083 --> 00:05:27.486
Which probe fixes it?

103
00:05:27.486 --> 00:05:28.555
Pause and think.

104
00:05:30.767 --> 00:05:34.112
A startup probe that allows more than ninety seconds.

105
00:05:34.112 --> 00:05:37.204
It holds liveness back until the app has started.

106
00:05:37.204 --> 00:05:38.467
Thanks for watching.
